.TH hello-python "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Hello Command"
.SH NAME
hello-python \- Print a greeting and a fresh UUID using a Python script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hello-python
.RI [ EXIT_CODE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B hello-python
is a minimal example command that runs a Python script with
.BR python3 (1)
from the
.I lib/python/
directory of the SMI toolset installation.
It demonstrates that Python scripting is correctly set up: the
.B python3
interpreter is on the PATH and the SMI library location is resolvable.
.PP
The command prints a greeting naming the current working directory and a fresh version 4 UUID,
then exits with the requested code.
The exit code is echoed on a second line only when it is non-zero.
It mirrors
.BR hello-elixir (1)
and
.BR hello-lisp (1),
exit code handling included.
.PP
The script is located by sourcing
.I python.sh
via
.BR smi-include (1),
which sets
.BR SMI_PYTHON_LIB_DIR .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I EXIT_CODE
Optional integer to exit with, in the range
.B 0
to
.BR 255 .
Defaults to
.B 0
when omitted.
An argument that is not an integer, or that falls outside that range, makes the command exit
.BR 1 .
Any further arguments are ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or no
.I EXIT_CODE
argument was given.
.TP
.B 1
The
.I EXIT_CODE
argument was not an integer in the range
.B 0
to
.BR 255 .
.TP
.I EXIT_CODE
The value passed on the command line, when valid.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $(smi-lib-location)/python/hello-python.py
The Python script executed by this command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR python3 (1),
.BR hello-elixir (1),
.BR hello-lisp (1),
.BR smi-include (1)
.SH AUTHOR
setmy.info
